  "outcome": "resolved",
  "reply_to_user": "Hi Leo, thanks for letting us know. I looked at your recent sign-ins. The Lisbon ones fit normal work travel: they happen at the start of your working day there, from the same network each time, and each one was approved on your usual phone with a single Duo prompt. I didn't see any warning signs, so you don't need to do anything and your account stays as it is. Someone from the help desk may call you on the number we have on file to confirm the trip, which is our standard practice. If you ever get a Duo prompt you didn't start, tap Deny and contact us straight away. Safe travels!",
  "internal_note": "Followed KB-031. User is Leo Martins (Sales, Account Executive, id 6, not VIP). Home time zone is America/New_York, usual hours 09:00-17:00. Sign-in logs for the last 14 days show New York sign-ins every weekday at about 09:02 ET through 10/06. Then 3 Lisbon, PT VPN sign-ins on 10/08, 10/09 and 10/10 at 09:05 Lisbon time (04:05 ET), all from the same residential IP 198.51.100.66, all successful with a Duo push. No failed passwords, no mfa_denied, no hosting or data center networks. No impossible travel: about 2 days pass between the last NY sign-in and the first Lisbon one. Duo is still enrolled on Leo's phone since 2025-12-15, so no new device was added. Account is active, 0 failed logins, not blocked. This pattern fits travel and no red flags are present, so no block or reset was requested and no escalation is needed. KB-031 step 1 says to confirm travel through a known channel. The user's statement only came in via the ticket, so a technician should do a quick callback to the number on file. If the user denies the trip on that call, reopen and request block_sign_in and reset_password.",
